From 7ca20d214eac2851b8ddafa8543e89f3c86fb181 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 17:26:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 098/206] feat(shorebird): document --no_pp_hash, and
 deliberately do not register it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Their help lists --no_pp_hash. Their own binary rejects it: the option parser
does not consume it, so it reaches Dart_SetVMFlags and comes back
"Unrecognized flags: pp_hash". Of the six Shorebird flags their help documents
beyond --shorebird, five are consumed by their parser and this one is not.

That was measured behaviourally, one flag at a time, and it had to be. The
obvious shortcut -- looking for the option name among the binary's strings --
cannot answer it: sdk_version is certainly registered and has no bare-name
string either. The control was run first and it came out negative, so the
string method was discarded rather than trusted.

Our binary already produced exactly their message for exactly that input
before this change, because neither of us registers it. So --no_pp_hash was
already reproduced behaviourally and was missing only its two lines of help.
Adding them completes it, and the withheld count drops from six flags to five.

⚠️ The comment says not to "fix" this by registering it, because a registered
--no_pp_hash would be a divergence rather than a feature.
analyze_snapshot_help_check.sh now enforces that: it requires both binaries to
reject the flag identically, and it fails if theirs ever starts accepting it.
Negative control: registering it here turns that check red.

This is the same species as print_dispatch_table_debug_info_to, and more
degenerate -- that flag is at least declared, and this one is help text with
nothing behind it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/bin/analyze_snapshot.cc b/runtime/bin/analyze_snapshot.cc
index adf4a13fe0f..15ad4b1b36b 100644
--- a/runtime/bin/analyze_snapshot.cc
+++ b/runtime/bin/analyze_snapshot.cc
@@ -58,19 +58,35 @@ static void PrintUsage() {
 "Shorebird options:                                                          \n"
 "--shorebird                                                                 \n"
 "  Produce Shorebird formatted JSON, requires --out.                         \n"
-// Shorebird lists six more flags at this point, in this order: --no_pp_hash,
+// Documented and deliberately NOT registered, because that is what theirs
+// does. Measured behaviourally rather than inferred: of the six Shorebird
+// flags their help lists beyond --shorebird, five are consumed by their option
+// parser and this one is not -- it falls through to Dart_SetVMFlags, which
+// rejects it with "Unrecognized flags: pp_hash". Our binary already produced
+// that same message for the same input before this line was added, so
+// reproducing --no_pp_hash faithfully means printing it and nothing else.
+//
+// ⚠️ Do not "fix" this by registering it. A registered --no_pp_hash would be a
+// divergence from them, not a feature.
+//
+// This is the same species as print_dispatch_table_debug_info_to, which is a
+// dead flag in their gen_snapshot, though more degenerate: that one is at
+// least declared, and this one is help text with nothing behind it.
+"--no_pp_hash                                                                \n"
+"  Ignore PP offsets when computing subgraph hashes.                         \n"
+// Shorebird lists five more flags at this point, in this order:
 // --dump_object_pool_link_data, --dump_blobs, --dump_object_pool,
 // --dump_class_table and --disassemble. They are deliberately NOT listed here,
 // because this binary does not implement them, and a help text advertising a
 // flag the parser rejects is worse than one that is short.
 //
 // The omission is pinned rather than left to memory:
-// analyze_snapshot_help_check.sh takes their --help, deletes exactly those six
+// analyze_snapshot_help_check.sh takes their --help, deletes exactly those five
 // blocks, and requires what is left to equal ours byte for byte. Every line
 // above is therefore checked against theirs on every run, and what is missing
 // is a number that has to shrink deliberately.
 //
-// None of the six is small. --dump_object_pool_link_data and --no_pp_hash need
+// None of the five is small. --dump_object_pool_link_data needs
 // op_subgraph_hash, a private subsystem this tree does not have --
 // ObjectPoolLinkInfo::WriteToStream is already here and byte-verified against
 // their files, so what is missing is the producer, not the format.
-- 
